# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the nightly OpenMP job (nightly.yml, "TSan OpenMP kernels").
#
# libomp's task records. "#pragma omp task firstprivate(...)" (src/sort_qs.h, the parallel quicksort) has libomp
# allocate a task record, and the generating thread copies the four captured values into it; whichever thread then
# runs the task reads them back. The hand-off between those two threads is a libomp-internal queue and pool
# operation, and libomp is uninstrumented, so TSan cannot see the ordering it establishes. Archer annotates the
# task creation and completion of the code it is handed, but not the record itself, so TSan reports the pair as a
# race on 8 bytes of a task record.
#
# Evidence (26 Nov 2026, clang 18.1.3 + libomp-18 + libarcher, -O1 -g -fsanitize=thread -fopenmp):
#   WARNING: ThreadSanitizer: data race
#     Write of size 8 at ... by thread T2:
#       #0 qs_rec_pard src/sort_qs.h:130:1         <- the firstprivate copies of a task just generated
#       #1 .omp_outlined..4 src/sort_qs.h:131:9
#     Previous read of size 8 at ... by thread T1:
#       #0 .omp_outlined..4 src/sort_qs.h:131:27   <- the task body reading the same firstprivate back
#     Location is heap block of size 1048576 allocated by thread T2:
#       #0 malloc
#       #1 libomp.so.5+0x1e035                     <- libomp's task-record pool, not Tessero's memory
# Neither stack touches the array being sorted, and the tasks' ranges are disjoint by construction (the partition
# and the pivots are the serial sort's), so the bytes reported are libomp bookkeeping and nothing else.
#
# race_top is deliberate: it matches the top frame only, so a race on the sorted array is still reported whenever
# the access is attributed to the function that performs it (qs_partition, qs_leaf*, qs_net*) rather than to the
# task prologue, the outlined task or parallel region body, or the parallel recursion's own frame. TSan matches
# suppression strings against function, file and module names only, and libomp's own frames are unsymbolized, so
# nothing narrower than this is available.
#
# Re-check when the runner's Archer grows its task-centric mode (LLVM, "use TSan fibers to implement task-centric
# analysis for OpenMP tasks", open as of 26 Nov 2026): with tasks mapped to TSan fibers the record accesses get
# their ordering from Archer, and these two rules can go.
race_top:qs_rec_par
race_top:.omp_outlined
